  "narrative": "All estimates are interpreted exactly as signed to the hypothesis direction. For these direction -1 claims, a positive estimate means PTEN-loss cells have a more negative dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency, than PTEN-intact cells. The central H81 finding, PTEN loss associated with stronger STAT3 dependency, was already independently validated and is now supported by several non-overlapping or partially overlapping internal contexts: batch A H84 +0.234 with interval +0.125 to +0.342; Cas9-activity-eligible H85 +0.224, +0.159 to +0.289; adherent culture H86 +0.262, +0.203 to +0.321; adenocarcinoma H87 +0.268, +0.206 to +0.330. These context estimates are smaller in point magnitude than the high-IFN subgroup but remain clearly positive, reasonably precise, and large relative to the STAT3 dependency distribution and the near-null specificity tests. I therefore accept them as dataset-level robustness findings and close them as completed context checks rather than as separate causal or generalizable claims.\n\nH90 tests the PTEN-loss main effect among PIK3CA-intact cells and is robust: +0.267, +0.210 to +0.323. H88 tests whether PIK3CA mutation modifies the PTEN-STAT3 effect and gives essentially zero interaction: +0.001, interval -0.150 to +0.152, with a small PIK3CA-mutant exposed cell_n of 38. Because the interval is wide and the point estimate is negligible, I reject the PIK3CA interaction claim as unsupported; this does not rule out a PIK3CA-mutant-specific main effect, but the registered interaction question is null. The specificity controls are informative negative results: H89 PTEN loss versus KRAS dependency +0.013 raw direction? The signed estimate is -0.013 and interval -0.035 to +0.008, so no meaningful KRAS-dependency association; H93 MDM2 specificity -0.015, -0.043 to +0.014; H94 USP7 specificity +0.017, -0.031 to +0.065; and H95 KRAS mutation versus STAT3 -0.018, -0.042 to +0.007. All are small, near or below the 10%-of-outcome clinical-significance scale, and interval-compatible with null or biologically negligible effects. They reduce the possibility that PTEN loss is simply a broad dependency-altering state and support specificity of the STAT3 finding.\n\nThe most scientifically interesting new result is the inflammatory context. H91 shows a very large PTEN-loss STAT3-dependency effect among high IFN-gamma signature cells: +0.423, +0.319 to +0.526. H92 is the corresponding interaction, showing that this effect is meaningfully larger in the high-IFN context than in the complement: +0.212, +0.094 to +0.331. The magnitude of the interaction is non-trivial relative to the main effect and the STAT3 dependency distribution, but it is more fragile because it comes from a subgroup and interaction contrast. I therefore accept H91 and H92 as dataset-level discoveries, close H91 as a completed context estimate, keep H92 active, and request independent validation of H92. This is the most valuable validation target because confirming context modification would be more important than re-confirming the already validated PTEN/STAT3 main effect. These remain associations in the supplied dataset and do not establish causality, pathway mechanism, or external generalizability.",
