  "narrative":
    "All estimates are interpreted exactly as signed to the claim direction. For these direction -1 dependency claims, a positive estimate means the exposed group has a more negative dependency score, i.e. stronger dependency, whereas a negative estimate means the opposite. H81 is reconfirmed at its validation response deadline. The claim is that PTEN-loss cells have stronger STAT3 dependency than PTEN-intact cells. Discovery was +0.267 with a 95% interval of +0.215 to +0.319, and independent validation was +0.310 with independent interval +0.231 to +0.388. The validation estimate is slightly larger, clearly positive, and precise enough to exclude null at the multiplicative interval level. Together with the accepted context checks in batch A, Cas9-activity-eligible samples, adherent culture, adenocarcinoma, PIK3CA-intact and PIK3CA-mutant contexts, high SLFN11, TP53-loss cells, and lower IFN context, this establishes PTEN loss as the strongest supported predictor of stronger STAT3 dependency in this dataset. It remains a dataset-level association, not a causal or generalizable statement.\n\nThe key new validation is H103, the median IFN-gamma interaction for PTEN loss and STAT3 dependency. Discovery gave +0.115 with interval +0.012 to +0.219, suggesting a modest positive interaction. Independent validation gave -0.0456 with interval -0.200 to +0.109. The validation estimate is opposite in sign, imprecise, and interval-compatible with null and moderate reverse effect. Combined with the failed replication of H92, where discovery gave +0.212 but independent validation gave only +0.041 with interval -0.140 to +0.222, the IFN interaction evidence is not robust. I therefore reject H103 as unsupported. H92 remains unresolved, but I defer further immediate work on IFN interactions because two different IFN splits failed to replicate and the high IFN main effect remains accepted as a subgroup estimate. The practical conclusion is that PTEN loss is broadly associated with stronger STAT3 dependency, but the data do not support a reproducible inflammatory IFN context modification.\n\nH3 is also reassessed and rejected. The overall EGFR-amplification main effect was +0.025 with interval including zero, and the adenocarcinoma and KRAS-intact subgroup estimates were also small and null. The effect is at or near a marginal scale and lacks internal replication, so it is better classified as a negative specificity result rather than an accepted EGFR dependency association.\n\nSupported core dataset associations are now: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency overall and in adenocarcinoma; TP53 wild-type with stronger USP7 dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. The validated core set is strong, whereas interaction evidence remains weak or unsupported. Important negative and specificity findings include: PTEN loss is not meaningfully associated with dependency_KRAS, MDM2, or USP7; SMARCA4 loss is not associated with STAT3 dependency; KRAS mutation is not associated with KRAS dependency after validation or with STAT3 or RIT1 in adenocarcinoma; MYC amplification among high MYC expression is not associated with KIF18A or RPL11; STK11 loss is not associated with KRAS dependency; PIK3CA, lineage, SLFN11, and IFN interaction tests are not supported.\n\nNext, the most informative analyses should shift from IFN interaction pursuit to testing whether accepted core effects are context-modified by other strong axes. I registered three new interactions: PTEN loss and STAT3 dependency within TP53-loss context to test attenuation of the PTEN effect independent of the p53/USP7 axis; SMARCA4 loss and SMARCA2 dependency within KEAP1-loss context to test independence from the KEAP1/NFE2L2 axis; and TP53-wild-type USP7 dependency within STK11-loss context to test independence from the STK11/LKB1 axis. I also deferred lower-priority null-like EGFR, ERBB2, KRAS, TMED10, MYC main-effect, and other exploratory claims so that the next analyses can focus on mechanistically targeted context checks. All conclusions remain internal to the supplied 2000-cell-line dataset.",
  "research_notes":
    "Core accepted and validated dataset associations: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency (H12); MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency overall and adenocarcinoma (H16, H21); TP53 wild-type -> stronger USP7 dependency (H15); KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency (H17); PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency overall (H81). PTEN/STAT3 main effect is robust across batch A, Cas9 eligibility, adherent culture, adenocarcinoma, PIK3CA-intact, PIK3CA-mutant, high SLFN11, TP53-loss, and lower-IFN contexts. IFN context modification is not supported: H92 top-quartile interaction failed independent validation, H103 median IFN interaction failed validation with opposite sign and is rejected. H92 remains unresolved but deferred; H91 remains an accepted high-IFN subgroup main effect, not evidence for interaction. H3 EGFR amp overall association rejected as small/null. Key negative specificity: PTEN loss not associated with KRAS/MDM2/USP7; SMARCA4 loss not associated with STAT3; KRAS mutation not associated with KRAS/STAT3/RIT1 in adeno; MYC high context not associated with KIF18A/RPL11; STK11 not associated with KRAS. Next priorities: new interactions PTEN/STAT3 x TP53-loss; SMARCA4/SMARCA2 x KEAP1-loss; TP53/USP7 x STK11-loss; plus remaining untested TP53-context main effects and interactions for accepted core axes (H31, H51, H32, H49, H64, H65, H66, H27, H24, H50, H71). All claims are dataset-level associations, not causal or generalizable."
